# Each phrase states an obligation without saying how strong it is, so
# the reader cannot tell a requirement from advice. The fix writes
# `must` for a requirement and `should` for advice, the grades the
# technical-writing skill defines, or a bare command for a step. When
# the subject is the system, the fix states what it does in the present
# tense (`the server needs to listen` becomes `the server listens`).
#
# `need to` skips a negation before it (`you do not need to restart`),
# which states that no obligation exists, and a determiner (a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`), where `need` is a
# noun (`the need to scale`). Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter
# consumes the word before `need` and narrows the finding back to the
# phrase. `If you need to X, do Y` still counts, because Weir cannot
# anchor a branch to the start of a sentence, and a branch that catches
# `You need to restart` also catches `if you need to`. The skill's
# condition-first form reads better anyway (`To X, do Y`), so the
# message names it.
#
# `it is important to` counts here except before the verbs that the
# Required filler rule reports (`it is important to note`). The passive
# rule lists `recommended` as a stative word, one that names a state, so
# `it is recommended that` draws one finding, from this rule. `you want
# to` is left out, because `If you want to X, do Y` is a fork the skill
# allows.
#
# Across the kubernetes and nix.dev docs, `need to` and its kin hit 558
# lines before the guards, and 7 of 10 sampled hits were vague
# obligations. All three misses were `If you want to`.
expr need [need, needs]
expr neg [not, never, don't, doesn't, didn't, DET]
expr wrap [expected, required, supposed]
expr verbs [note, remember, understand, mention, realize, recognize]
expr main [<(!@neg @need to), (@need to)>, ([is, are] @wrap to), ([is, are] recommended), <([(it is), it's] important to !@verbs), ([(it is), it's] important to)>, (it is advisable), (it is a good idea), (you'll want to), (you will want to), ([may, might] want to), (be sure to), (make sure to), (ought to), (must try to), shall]
let message "Vague obligation. Write must or should for what a person does, the present tense for what the system does, or open with To and the goal."
let description "Flags needs to, is expected to, is recommended, and other phrases that hide how strong an obligation is."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "You do not need to restart the node."
allows "The plan covers the need to scale reads."
allows "It is important to note the version."
allows "If you want to pin the version, set the tag."
allows "A timeout is required for every request."
allows "You must pin the version."
